## Federal statutory authority

The federal regulation I reviewed repeatedly describes these as **home and community-based services furnished under a waiver** and states that the waiver request is made “**under this section**,” referring to the HCBS waiver authority implemented in **42 CFR Part 441, Subpart G**. The controlling statutory authority for that type of waiver is **section 1915(c) of the Social Security Act**.

A related federal regulation also confirms that HCBS waiver services are services furnished “**under a waiver granted under the provisions of part 441, subpart G**.” See **42 CFR 440.180 — "Home and community-based waiver services"**.  
(That regulation supports the connection to the HCBS waiver framework, but the waiver application requirements themselves are in 42 CFR 441.301–.303.) [42 CFR Part 441](https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-42/part-441)

## What the state must demonstrate in its waiver application

Under **42 CFR 441.301 — "Contents of request for a waiver"**, the waiver request must include:

- the **assurances** required by **42 CFR 441.302** and the **supporting documentation** required by **42 CFR 441.303**;
- if applicable, requests for waivers of certain Medicaid requirements, including:
  - **statewideness**,
  - **comparability of services**, and
  - certain **income and resource rules** for medically needy individuals in the community;
- a statement explaining whether the agency will **refuse HCBS to a beneficiary when it can reasonably expect the cost of waiver services would exceed the cost of equivalent institutional care**;
- provisions showing the services will be furnished:
  - under a **written person-centered service plan** approved by the Medicaid agency,
  - **only to beneficiaries who are not inpatients** of a hospital, nursing facility, or ICF/IID, and
  - **only to beneficiaries who, absent waiver services, would require** the Medicaid-covered level of care provided in a hospital, nursing facility, or ICF/IID;
- a description of:
  - who will develop the plan of care,
  - the **target group(s)** to be served, and
  - the **services**, with each service separately defined;
- documentation that the **individual evaluation requirements** in 42 CFR 441.303(c) will be met.  
See **42 CFR 441.301 — "Contents of request for a waiver"**. [42 CFR Part 441](https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-42/part-441)

## Core assurances the state must provide

Under **42 CFR 441.302 — "State assurances"**, CMS will not approve a waiver unless the state gives satisfactory assurances including:

- **Health and welfare safeguards** for beneficiaries;
- adequate **provider standards**;
- compliance with applicable **state licensure/certification** requirements;
- if serving more than one target group in a single waiver, assurance that the state can meet the **unique service needs** of those groups;
- assurance that services are provided in **home and community-based settings** meeting federal requirements;
- assurance that the state operates an **incident management system** for critical incidents;
- **financial accountability** for waiver funds and independent audit requirements;
- that the state will provide for **evaluation and reevaluation** of need;
- that beneficiaries will be **informed of feasible alternatives** and allowed to choose either institutional services or HCBS;
- that the average per capita cost under the waiver will not exceed the average per capita cost of institutional care and other Medicaid services that would have been provided **without the waiver**;
- that the agency will maintain a **written plan of care**;
- that the Medicaid agency will provide for **review of the need for care**; and
- that claims will be made only for **properly authorized services**.  
See **42 CFR 441.302 — "State assurances"**. [42 CFR Part 441](https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-42/part-441)

## Supporting documentation the application must include

Under **42 CFR 441.303 — "Supporting documentation required"**, the state must furnish enough information to support those assurances, including:

- a description of the **safeguards** protecting health and welfare;
- a description of the **records and information** maintained to support financial accountability;
- a description of the state’s plan for **evaluation and reevaluation**, including:
  - who performs the evaluations,
  - how they are conducted,
  - the evaluation form used,
  - procedures for written documentation, and
  - procedures for reevaluations at regular intervals;
- a description of how the state will **inform eligible beneficiaries of feasible alternatives** and allow them to choose between institutional services and HCBS;
- an explanation of how the state will apply the **post-eligibility treatment of income and resources** rules for waiver participants eligible under a special income level;
- an explanation, with supporting documentation satisfactory to CMS, of how the state estimated **average per capita expenditures**;
- a demonstration that:
  - **D + D′ ≤ G + G′**, meaning the estimated annual average per capita cost of waiver services plus other Medicaid services for waiver participants must be **less than or equal to** the estimated annual average per capita institutional and other Medicaid costs that would occur absent the waiver;
- the number of **unduplicated beneficiaries** the state intends to serve each year, which becomes a cap unless increased through an approved amendment.  
See **42 CFR 441.303 — "Supporting documentation required"**. [42 CFR Part 441](https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-42/part-441)

## Plain-language summary

For NC’s **CAP/DA waiver**, the state’s waiver application must basically show CMS that:

1. **The waiver is legally authorized under section 1915(c).**
2. **Participants would otherwise need institutional-level care** absent waiver services.
3. **Services will be delivered under a person-centered plan of care.**
4. **Beneficiary health and welfare will be protected.**
5. **Providers meet standards and oversight requirements.**
6. **Participants are informed of alternatives** and can choose HCBS instead of institutional care.
7. **The waiver is cost-neutral** in the sense required by federal regulation: average waiver costs cannot exceed average institutional and related Medicaid costs that would otherwise be incurred.
8. The state has adequate **documentation, evaluation, reevaluation, financial accountability, and program limits/capacity information**.

